import{_ as t,o as i,c as n,a2 as a}from"./chunks/framework.CdjpmwdR.js";const s="/assets/choreography.Dua7ONdj.svg",o="/assets/orchestration.OprR4jGj.svg",m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Agents Are Changing How Services Connect","description":"If the Provider keeps OpenAPI unchanged, Agent + Skill may take over part of the client-side integration logic and make service-to-service calls more semantic.","frontmatter":{"category":"node/agentic","date":"2026-06-08","title":"Agents Are Changing How Services Connect","description":"If the Provider keeps OpenAPI unchanged, Agent + Skill may take over part of the client-side integration logic and make service-to-service calls more semantic.","tags":["node/agentic","OpenAPI","Skill","integration"],"image":"/blogs/assets/202606-agent-changes-service-interconnection/choreography.svg"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/blogs/2026/202606-agent-changes-service-interconnection.md","filePath":"en/blogs/2026/202606-agent-changes-service-interconnection.md"}'),r={name:"en/blogs/2026/202606-agent-changes-service-interconnection.md"};function l(c,e,h,d,p,g){return i(),n("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a('<p>After building the multica + brains symbiotic system, I started asking the reverse question: how should Agent applications call one another? Each of them already has Agent modules and can use Agent capability internally. At the same time, I also want them to connect to each other, and even be managed in a unified way by a front-end Agent such as openclaw. Once you move in that direction, standardizing call relationships — and constraining, planning, and designing the boundaries of large and small Agent capabilities — becomes a real problem.</p><p>In the past, microservices and complex systems already gave us a relatively mature interconnection pattern: the Service Provider exposes OpenAPI, the Service Client makes the calls, and then stitches multiple interfaces into a business flow. That pattern works well, but it lacks business semantics. The caller still has to write a lot of custom logic.</p><p>If we leave the API with the Provider, put business rules into Skills, and let Agents act as Clients, service-to-service calls may start to look different.</p><h2 id="in-the-traditional-pattern-the-problem-is-not-the-api-itself" tabindex="-1">In the traditional pattern, the problem is not the API itself <a class="header-anchor" href="#in-the-traditional-pattern-the-problem-is-not-the-api-itself" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;In the traditional pattern, the problem is not the API itself&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The classic pattern is clear: the Service Provider exposes OpenAPI, the Service Client generates or writes call code based on that interface definition, then combines multiple interfaces into a business flow. There is nothing wrong with that model. What it solves is standardized invocation.</p><p>But the gap is equally clear: <strong>an API defines a capability boundary. It does not define business rules.</strong> Even HATEOAS or GraphQL are better at resource access and query expression than at directly carrying rules like this: when the order amount is below 100, check inventory first, then recommend an item that works well as an impulse add-on.</p><p>Of course, that logic could always be implemented before. It just usually required a lot of custom code to stitch together multiple APIs, business rules, exception handling, and flow decisions.</p><h2 id="agents-let-the-caller-work-with-business-rules" tabindex="-1">Agents let the caller work with business rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#agents-let-the-caller-work-with-business-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Agents let the caller work with business rules&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Since Agents appeared, I have increasingly felt that application interconnection may gain a new implementation style:</p><ul><li><strong>The Provider continues to expose APIs</strong></li><li><strong>Skills add business rules, user use cases, the business relationship between multiple APIs, and invocation strategy</strong></li><li><strong>Agents act as the calling side, load those skills, then understand and invoke the capabilities</strong></li></ul><p>In rough terms, you can think of it like this:</p><blockquote><p><strong>OpenAPI ≈ Skill + API</strong></p></blockquote><p>I am not trying to replace OpenAPI. The point is closer to this: <strong>traditional OpenAPI mainly answers “can it be called,” while Skills add “how should it be called in a business context.”</strong></p><p>What a Skill can contain is not just interface descriptions. It can also include:</p><ul><li>user use cases</li><li>the business meaning of one or more APIs and the relationship between them</li><li>business-level calling rules</li><li>technical exception-handling strategies</li></ul><p>That means the Service Provider may barely need to change. The side that changes most is actually the Client. A large amount of integration logic that used to live in code may move into Prompt + Skill driven invocation and orchestration.</p><h2 id="two-modes-choreography-and-orchestration" tabindex="-1">Two modes: Choreography and Orchestration <a class="header-anchor" href="#two-modes-choreography-and-orchestration" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Two modes: Choreography and Orchestration&quot;">​</a></h2><p>I think this can be understood through two different modes: Choreography and Orchestration.</p><p><img src="'+s+'" alt="Choreography: multiple Agent Clients use Skills to call three service APIs through CLI/MCP"></p><p>In <strong>Choreography</strong>, there is no central orchestrator. Each application or service still collaborates like a microservice system, but the Service Client is no longer just hard-coded logic. Instead, Agent + Skill can take over that role, so the invocation becomes more semantic and more business-oriented.</p><p><img src="'+o+'" alt="Orchestration: a Supervisor Agent uses Skills to orchestrate multiple service APIs through CLI/MCP"></p><p>In <strong>Orchestration</strong>, I find the picture even more interesting: the central coordinator could be implemented entirely by an Agent. In other words, a Supervisor Agent can take the necessary context, then autonomously call multiple applications and services to complete integration flows that used to require a large amount of Client-side code.</p><p>Calling this “zero-code development” would obviously be exaggerated. A more accurate way to say it is: once the Service Provider already exposes standard APIs, <strong>the customized integration code on the Client side may be significantly reduced or partially replaced by Agent + Skill.</strong></p><h2 id="this-is-the-framing-i-currently-prefer" tabindex="-1">This is the framing I currently prefer <a class="header-anchor" href="#this-is-the-framing-i-currently-prefer" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;This is the framing I currently prefer&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This is still an early personal idea, but I increasingly prefer to look at it this way: <strong>an Agent is not just an intelligent module added to an existing application. It may also become the next generation of Service Client.</strong></p><p>If that direction holds, then in the future what applications expose to one another may not be only interfaces, but also capabilities and business rules.</p><h2 id="needs-verification" tabindex="-1">Needs Verification <a class="header-anchor" href="#needs-verification" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Needs Verification&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>The minimal reusable structure of a <code>Skill</code> still needs more practical validation.</li><li>We need clearer boundaries for which client-side integration logic is suitable to be replaced by Agents and which parts should still remain in code.</li><li>The reliability, auditability, and failure-handling model of a Supervisor Agent in real production scenarios still need more samples.</li></ul>',28)])])}const b=t(r,[["render",l]]);export{m as __pageData,b as default};
